.TH STURY 1 "2026-11-11" "stury 0.1.1" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
stury \- JSON and CSV codec with a jq 1.8.2\-compatible command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stury
[\-bcCMnRrsSaejV] [\-\-tab|\-\-indent n] [\-\-arg name value] [\-\-argjson name value] [\-\-slurpfile name file] [\-\-rawfile name file] [\-f file] [\-L dir] [\-\-seq] [\-\-stream|\-\-stream\-errors] [\-\-from format] [\-\-to format] [\-\-grow\-header] [csv options] [\-\-max\-depth n] [\-\-follow] [\-\-explain] [yaml and xml options] [stury options] <jq filter> [file...]
.br
.B stury
.B check
[\-\-from format|\-\-seq] [\-\-max\-depth n] [file...]
.br
.B stury
.B check
\-\-schema file [\-\-schema\-ref dir] [\-\-schema\-draft d] [\-\-format\-assert] [\-\-max\-errors n] [\-\-report lines|basic|problem] [file...]
.br
.B stury
.B check
\-\-filter <jq filter> \-\-schema file [\-\-from format] [\-nsR] [\-\-arg name value] [\-c]
.br
.B stury
fmt
[\-\-from format] [\-\-to format] [\-\-in\-place|\-\-check] [\-c|\-\-indent n|\-\-tab] [\-\-max\-depth n] [file...]
.br
.B stury
fmt
\-\-schema file [\-\-to format] [\-\-schema\-ref dir] [\-\-schema\-draft d] [\-\-max\-errors n] [\-\-format\-assert] [file...]
.br
.B stury
edit
[\-\-from format|\-\-seq] [\-\-in\-place|\-\-check|\-\-diff] [\-\-max\-depth n] [\-f file] [\-L dir] [\-\-arg name value] <jq filter> [file...]
.br
.B stury
diff
[\-\-from format] [\-\-set|\-\-position] [\-\-key [path=]member] [\-\-facts] [\-\-json] old new
.br
.B stury
diff
\-\-old\-schema file \-\-new\-schema file [\-\-compat backward|forward|full] [\-\-json]
.br
.B stury
diff
\-\-shape [\-\-from format] old new
.br
.B stury
schema infer
[\-\-from format] [\-\-infer] [\-\-ranges] [layout options] [file...]
.br
.B stury
schema sample
[\-\-to format] [\-\-count n] [\-\-seed n] [\-\-invalid] [\-\-comments] [\-\-schema\-ref dir] [\-\-schema\-draft d] [layout options] schema
.br
.B stury
schema gen
[\-\-package name] [\-\-format\-assert] [\-\-schema\-ref dir] [\-\-schema\-draft d] schema
.br
.B stury
\-h | \-V | \-\-build\-configuration
.SH DESCRIPTION
stury applies a jq 1.8 filter to JSON inputs and gives jq's results and exit codes. Its messages start with stury: and are worded its own way. It accepts all of jq 1.8's options except \-\-run\-tests, \-\-debug\-dump\-disasm and \-\-debug\-trace, a test\-file runner and bytecode listings it does not have. The filter language and those options are described in
.BR jq (1).
This page covers stury's own options and how its input handling differs from jq's.
.PP
.B stury check
(or
.BR validate )
validates its inputs, accepting any input that
.B stury .
reads without error, and prints nothing when they are valid. Every file is judged, and each file's first fault is reported. It requires at least one JSON text: an empty or whitespace-only input is "No JSON text" and exits 5, except under \-\-seq, where it is no records and passes. It refuses the options fmt refuses, and \-\-indent and \-\-tab. It is the subcommand when it is the first argument that is not an option. With no file on a terminal it prints the short usage and exits 2. \fBstury check \-\-help\fR lists its options.
.PP
With \fB\-\-schema\fR \fIfile\fR, check also judges each input against the JSON Schema in \fIfile\fR (drafts 4 to 2020\-12) and tells every failure as file:line:col: message at path, on stderr, exiting 1 when an input parses and fails the schema. \fB\-\-schema\-ref\fR \fIdir\fR sets the directory a file reference may read, \fB\-\-schema\-draft\fR \fId\fR names the draft of a schema with no $schema (4, 6, 7, 2019\-09 or 2020\-12, the default; diff and schema sample and gen take it and \-\-schema\-ref too), \fB\-\-format\-assert\fR makes format fail, \fB\-\-max\-errors\fR \fIn\fR keeps at most n failures of an input, and \fB\-\-report\fR \fIlines|basic|problem\fR writes the JSON Schema basic output or an RFC 9457 problem document on standard output instead. NDJSON and \-\-seq records are checked one by one, and for csv and tsv the schema describes the table, an array of rows. docs/cli.md has the details.
.PP
With \fB\-\-filter\fR \fIfilter\fR, check types the jq filter against the schema of its inputs and reads no input. It refuses each step no input the schema describes can take, a member no object of the schema describes among them, as a compile error is told, and writes the JSON Schema every output of the filter passes on standard output. It exits 1 after a refusal, else 0. \-n, \-s, \-R, \-\-from csv or tsv and the variables shape what the filter is typed on. docs/cli.md has the details.
.PP
.B stury fmt
writes each input back in the printer's layout, or in another format: it runs no filter, reads each file as
\-\-from and writes it as \-\-to (the same format by default). JSON and JSONC are laid out as stury prints them, comments kept; ndjson is
one compact value per line. Between these formats only the layout changes: each number, string and key is written
as it was, and every member of an object is kept, a repeated key included. csv and tsv are a header and one record
per row, re-spelled by the cell writer, each file a table of its own. A leading byte order mark is not written. A JSON text whose root is an array is
written to csv or tsv as a list of rows. Every file is formatted, and each file's first fault is reported. The run is
idempotent, and a file already in that layout comes back byte for byte. \-c is \-\-indent 0. \fBstury fmt \-\-help\fR lists its options.
.PP
With \fB\-\-schema\fR \fIfile\fR, fmt judges each file against the JSON Schema as check \-\-schema does (a csv or tsv cell by \-\-infer, never by the schema) and formats a file that passes as plain fmt does. A file that fails is told as check \-\-schema tells it, with at most \-\-max\-errors n failures, is not written, and makes the exit 1. A table written is judged again as it reads back, under \-\-infer when the input is no table, which quotes a text that reads as a number, true or false, and a failure there is told against \fIname\fR (output). \-\-in\-place and \-\-check leave a file with a failure as it is.
.PP
.B stury schema infer
writes the JSON Schema every record of its inputs passes: the kinds met at each position, a member required where every object had it, an empty csv cell a null. \-\-ranges also keeps the least and greatest number and length. An input that does not parse, an empty one, and one whose schema would nest past the depth a JSON text may have exit 5.
.PP
.B stury schema sample
writes \-\-count n values (default 1) that pass the schema, chosen by \-\-seed n, a text a line in the format \-\-to names; \-\-invalid writes values that fail it, \-\-comments writes descriptions as comments where the format has them, and under \-\-to csv or tsv \-\-infer reads the sample's cells as check \-\-infer does. A schema for which no passing value is found, or under \-\-invalid no failing one, exits 1 with the file named.
.PP
.B stury schema gen
writes the Odin source of the types a model reads the schema's values with, and a register procedure for the rules no tag holds; \-\-package names the package. A keyword no Odin type spells exits 1, told with the path of the node that holds it.
.PP
.B stury edit
runs the filter on each input and splices its outputs into the input's own bytes: every byte the edit does not touch
stays, and the identity run is byte for byte. A value taken from the input keeps its spelling, a built one is written
by jq's writer, and a new member or element follows its container's line, indent and spacing. Members and elements take
the output's order, a moved one keeping its bytes and comments. Each input is written back in its own format: JSON,
JSONC and JSON5 as one text, which takes exactly one output (exit 5 otherwise); CSV and TSV, NDJSON, \-\-seq and \-R
lines record by record, where a record with no output is dropped, each output past the first is a new record after
it, and a table's column order, new and dropped columns change the header and every row. \-s runs the filter once on
the array of the records. A dropped member takes its comments, and of a repeated key the last member is edited.
Every file is edited, and each file's first fault is reported. \-a, \-S, \-\-indent and \-\-tab write built values; not
with \-\-to, \-n, \-\-stream, \-\-stream\-errors, \-\-follow, \-r, \-j, \-\-raw\-output0 or \-C.
\fBstury edit \-\-help\fR lists its options.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-from " format"
Read json (the default), jsonc, json5, ndjson, csv, tsv, toml, yaml, xml or cbor. With check, judge the input as that format is read.
.TP
.BI \-\-to " format"
Write json (the default), jsonc, ndjson (as \-c), csv, tsv, toml, yaml, xml or cbor. toml, yaml and xml write each output as a text of the format, which a node of a toml or yaml input, and an xml document written whole, keeps its comments and spellings in, and cbor each output as one item in preferred serialization with nothing between the items. toml takes an object only and no JSON layout option; yaml takes \-c (flow style), \-\-indent and \-a. xml takes an object of one member, the root element, and \-c, \-\-indent and \-a. A member is an element, an array repeats it, and #text is text. csv and tsv write each output as one record. An object is a row under a header of the first object's keys (sorted under \-S). A missing key is an empty cell, and an extra key ends the run with exit 5. An array or a scalar is a row with no header, and the two kinds do not mix. A string is its text, a number is spelled as it was written, and null is an empty cell. csv writes "" as a quoted empty cell; tsv cannot tell the two apart. An array or object in a cell ends the run (tojson makes text of it). Not with \-r, \-j, \-a, \-C, \-c, \-\-indent, \-\-tab, \-\-seq or \-\-raw\-output0, and not with check.
.TP
.B \-\-grow\-header
With \-\-to csv or tsv, hold the output and write a header of every key in the order first seen (byte order under \-S). Memory holds all the output.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " ndjson"
Read one JSON text per line, not with \-R or \-\-seq. \-\-to ndjson prints compact, one value per line, so \-\-indent, \-\-tab and \-\-seq are refused with it.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " jsonc"
Read JSONC (comments and trailing commas), also for \-\-argjson, \-\-jsonargs, \-\-slurpfile (one document per file) and fromjson. Values print with their comments. Not with \-R, \-\-seq or \-\-stream, and \-\-to jsonc not with \-\-seq.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " json5"
Read JSON5 1.0.0: each input is one text, read as written (comments kept), and numbers print in JSON's spelling. Infinity and NaN end the run, having no JSON value. Read only: not \-\-to json5, nor fmt \-\-in\-place or fmt \-\-check. \-\-argjson, \-\-slurpfile, \-\-jsonargs and fromjson read JSON5. Not with \-R, \-\-seq or \-\-stream.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " toml"
Read TOML 1.1, one document per input (an empty input is {}). A datetime is a string that .@tag names, and .@style tells a string's quoting and a number's form. Not with \-R, \-\-seq, \-\-stream or \-\-follow.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " yaml"
Read YAML 1.2 with the core schema, each document of a stream one input. A repeated key, an alias to no anchor and a collection as a key are parse errors. .@tag, .@anchor, .@alias and .@style read a node's properties. edit edits each document as a record. Not with \-R, \-\-seq or \-\-stream.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " xml"
Read XML 1.0 with its internal subset, one document per input: an element is a member named as written, its text a string (\-\-infer types it) or a #text member. .&name reads an attribute by its name as written, and .@attrs, .@name, .@namespace, .@content, .@pi and .@doctype the element's attributes, name, namespace and text and the document's instructions and doctype. A name an element repeats is an array of its elements. edit keeps every byte the output does not change. Not with \-R, \-\-seq, \-\-stream or \-\-follow.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " cbor"
Read CBOR (RFC 8949), each item of an RFC 8742 sequence one input, in any serialization. A byte string reads as its base64, a byte string key as its bytes, an integer key as its decimal text and a tag as its content, whose number .@tag reads. edit edits each item as a record. Not with \-R, \-\-seq, \-\-stream or \-\-follow.
.TP
.BI \-\-alias\-limit " n"
With \-\-from yaml, refuse aliases that read more than n bytes again (default ten times the text, at least 1 MiB).
.TP
.B \-\-merge\-keys
With \-\-from yaml, read a << member as a merge key.
.TP
.BI \-\-expansion\-limit " n"
With \-\-from xml, refuse entity references that expand to more than n bytes in all.
.TP
.B \-\-unread\-entities
With \-\-from xml, read a reference to an entity no declaration stury read names as its own text.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " csv"
Read CSV (RFC 4180). The first record of each file names the fields. Each later record is one input, an object of those names with each cell's text as a string, never a number. An empty cell with no quotes is null and a quoted "" is ""; every empty tsv cell is null. A repeated header name, a record of another width and a fault in the text end the file with NAME:LINE:COLUMN: FAULT and exit 5. A regular file is read whole and a pipe a window at a time, a window holding the longest record. A record of another width is told at the byte where its width is wrong, as having more or fewer fields than the first record. A filter that is only columns (.name, .a, .b, or [.a, .b] under \-c) is printed from the cells in pieces on every core. With \-s the records are one array. Not with \-R, \-\-seq or \-\-stream.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " tsv"
Read tab\-separated values as \-\-from csv does, with no quoting and the escapes \\t, \\n, \\r, \\0 and \\\\ that jq's @tsv writes.
.TP
.B \-\-in\-place
With \fBfmt\fR or \fBedit\fR, rewrite each file through a temporary file in its directory and a rename. Mode (and owner and group where permitted) are kept; other hard links keep the old bytes. Only when \-\-to is \-\-from; refused with \-\-check, with standard input, and on a non\-regular file (exit 2).
.TP
.B \-\-check
With \fBfmt\fR or \fBedit\fR, write nothing to stdout: name each file whose formatted or edited bytes differ from
its bytes on stderr and exit 1, or 0 when none would change. A fault exits 5.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-depth " n"
Refuse nesting past n levels (default 1024; jq's is 10000).
.TP
.BI \-\-delimiter " c"
With \-\-from csv or tsv, split cells at c, one ASCII character (\et for a tab).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-header
With \-\-from csv or tsv, read the first record as data: each record is an array of its cells.
.TP
.BI \-\-comment " c"
With \-\-from csv or tsv, a line whose first byte is c is no record.
.TP
.B \-\-infer
With \-\-from csv or tsv, an unquoted cell that is exactly a JSON number, true or false reads as that value. A quoted cell stays a string. With \-\-from xml, a text value reads the same way.
.TP
.B \-\-ragged
With \-\-from csv or tsv, read records of any width: a narrower one lacks its missing columns, and a wider one's extra cells are named by their position from 1.
.TP
.B \-\-lazy\-quotes
With \-\-from csv, a quote inside an unquoted cell is text.
.TP
.BI \-\-out\-delimiter " c"
With \-\-to csv or tsv, split written cells at c. A table written as the format read keeps the read's delimiter by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-out\-comment " c"
With \-\-to csv or tsv, start written comment lines with c. A table written from a table keeps the read's comment character by default, and fmt of a table into a table keeps its comment lines when the output has one.
.TP
.B \-\-out\-no\-header
With \-\-to csv or tsv, write no header line.
.TP
.B \-\-crlf
With \-\-to csv or tsv, end each written record with CR LF.
.TP
.B \-\-follow
With one named file, answer each record as it is appended, like tail \-f FILE | stury FILTER without the pipe. It reads NDJSON, \-\-seq, \-R, jq's texts per file, CSV, TSV and YAML streams. A record cut short waits for its delimiter, the output is written after each record, and a file that shrinks is read again from its start with a note on stderr. SIGINT and SIGTERM end the run once the answers so far are written, by the signal, as they end jq. Not with standard input, several files, \-s, \-n, \-\-stream, JSONC, JSON5, TOML, XML, CBOR, check or fmt.
.TP
.B \-\-explain
Write how the filter runs and what the run did to stderr, as lines \fBstury: explain: KEY: SENTENCE\fR, and run the filter as without it. Before the run: filter, input, plan (reading only a path, reading only named columns, or building each input), because (each reason an input is built), decides (what the speed and memory follow). After it: ran (inputs read by path and built), threads, fallback (the first input a path plan built after all, and why), time and used (peak memory, bytes read and written). The keys and reasons are listed in docs/cli.md. Not with check or fmt.
.TP
.B \-V
Print stury\- and the version it was built with: a release version, or the short commit hash from a source build (dev outside a checkout).
.SH INPUT DIFFERENCES FROM JQ
A number prints as it was written (1E2 stays 1E2), where jq prints 1E+2. A file of 1 MiB or more is answered on every core when the filter works one record or entry at a time, with the output of a serial run.
.PP
stury reads strict RFC 8259 JSON. It rejects, with exit 5, these inputs that jq accepts: 01, 1., NaN, nan, Infinity, invalid UTF\-8 in a string, a lone low surrogate escape, a NUL after a value, and nesting past \-\-max\-depth. A parse error names the fault and the position of the byte where it was found. Each file ends its texts, except under \-\-seq. The full list is in docs/cli.md, "Differences from jq".
.SH EXIT STATUS
As jq: 1 under \-e when the last output is false or null, 2 for a usage or file error or output that cannot be written (a closed stdout included, except under check, fmt \-\-check and fmt \-\-in\-place), 3 for a filter that does not compile, 4 under \-e when there is no output, 5 for invalid input or an error on the last input. \fBfmt \-\-check\fR and \fBedit \-\-check\fR exit 1 when a file would change, and \fBdiff\fR when the inputs differ or compatibility is not proven. \fBcheck \-\-schema\fR and \fBfmt \-\-schema\fR exit 1 when an input parses and fails the schema, and 5 when one does not parse; \fBcheck \-\-filter\fR exits 1 after a refusal. \fBschema sample\fR and \fBschema gen\fR exit 1 for a schema they cannot answer, and \fBschema infer\fR 5 for an input it cannot describe. A schema that does not compile is exit 2.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jq (1),
docs/cli.md in stury's source.
